# NOETHERVIM: LATEX

This lesson needs the `languages/latex` bundle and a working TeX
installation with `latexmk`. Enable it with `<Space>cb`, restart, and run
`:checkhealth noethervim`, which reports the LaTeX toolchain once the
bundle is on.

Most of what follows is buffer-local to `.tex` files, so open a real
document to try any of it.

Table of contents:

- [Compiling](*compile*)
- [The PDF](*pdf*)
- [Moving around a document](*nav*)
- [Labels and references](*labels*)
- [Writing faster](*writing*)
- [Spelling in LaTeX](*spell*)
- [Big projects](*projects*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## COMPILING *compile*

`<LocalLeader>` is `,` by default, and VimTeX's commands live under
`<LocalLeader>l`.

    `,ll`   compile
    `,lk`   stop compiling
    `,le`   the error and warning list
    `,lt`   the table of contents

The compile model is worth stating, because it differs from VimTeX's own
default. `,ll` compiles **once** rather than leaving a process watching the
file. After that first compile, saving the file recompiles it. So the habit
is: `,ll` once when you sit down, then just write and save.

`:VimtexStop` ends compilation and disarms that on-save rebuild; the next
manual `,ll` re-arms it. `:VimtexStopAll` does it for every open project.

Documents are compiled with LuaLaTeX, which handles `fontspec` and unicode
mathematics that pdflatex cannot. Both that and the one-shot behavior are
ordinary `vim.g` values you can override in `lua/user/plugins/`.

Fill in the key that compiles the document:

Compile this document: ___

## THE PDF *pdf*

    `,lv`   view the PDF at the cursor position
    `,lf`   toggle the PDF following the cursor as you move
    `:PDF`  open the compiled PDF in the system viewer
    `yP`    put the compiled PDF on the system clipboard

`,lv` is forward search: it asks your viewer to jump to the line you are
on. It resolves against the **last build**, so if the PDF is behind the
buffer it says so rather than sending you to the wrong place.

`,lf` is the one to try if you have a second monitor. The viewer tracks
your cursor until you press it again.

`:PDF`, `yP` and Oil's `gP` treat the PDF as a file and need no viewer
configured. `,lv` is the one that drives your viewer and syncs with it.

Inverse search, clicking in the PDF to jump to the source, is set up by the
bundle; which viewer you use decides how it is triggered.

## MOVING AROUND A DOCUMENT *nav*

Structural motions, on the bracket pair as usual:

    `]g` `[g`   next and previous theorem environment
    `]p` `[p`   proofs
    `]x` `[x`   examples
    `]P` `]X`   the matching `\end` of a theorem or example

There is also a paragraph text object that understands LaTeX rather than
blank lines:

    `ig` `ag`   a paragraph, stopping at display math, environments,
              `\item` and sectioning

So `dag` deletes a paragraph without swallowing the equation after it,
which `dap` would.

Two pickers for jumping by name:

    `,w`          every label and heading in the project
    `,vw`         the word count

## LABELS AND REFERENCES *labels*

This is the part that saves the most time in a long document.

    `<C-]>`   jump to the label under the cursor, through the tag stack
    `<C-t>`   come back
    `gd`      the same jump, without the tag stack

Put the cursor on a `\cref{thm:main}`, `\ref{...}` or `\eqref{...}` and
`<C-]>` takes you to where that label is defined, **across subfiles and
nested sub-books**. `<C-t>` returns, and the two nest, so you can follow a
chain of references and walk back out of it.

Completion knows your labels too: typing inside `\cref{` offers them with
the theorem statement as documentation, so you pick the right one by
reading it rather than by remembering the key.

If a label you just wrote is not offered yet, `,vul` rebuilds the label
cache and `,vuh` the heading cache.

Fill in the key that jumps to a label and can be returned from:

Jump to a label, through the tag stack: ___

## WRITING FASTER *writing*

    `<S-CR>`     insert mode: continue the structure you are in, closing
               environments and continuing `\item` lists
    `,P`         paste an image from the clipboard as a figure
    `<C-S-c>`    insert mode: a citation from your `.bib` file
    `@`          at the start of a line above `\begin{document}`, complete
               a preamble fragment
    `.`          on a line of its own in a `.tex` file, complete an image
               from `./images/` into a full figure block

`<S-CR>` needs a terminal that sends it distinctly from `<CR>`; kitty,
WezTerm, Ghostty and most GUIs do, Apple Terminal does not.
`:checkhealth noethervim` guesses for your terminal.

The `writing/zotero` bundle adds `,z`, a citation picker over a local
Zotero library, if that is where your references live.

Snippets are where most of the typing saving is, and they have their own
lesson: [lesson eleven](@tutor:noethervim-11-snippets).

## SPELLING IN LATEX *spell*

Spellcheck understands LaTeX rather than treating macros as misspellings.

    `zg`   add the word under the cursor to your dictionary
    `zw`   mark it wrong
    `z=`   suggestions

On `K\"ahler` these read the decoded word rather than the fragment, so you
are adding "Kähler" and not `"ahler`.

The capitalization operators know LaTeX too. `gZ` on `\emph{the cat}` gives
`\emph{The cat}` rather than capitalizing the macro name.

## BIG PROJECTS *projects*

For a document split across files with `subfiles.cls`, a buffer starts in
**local mode**: compiling from a chapter builds that chapter's own PDF, not
the whole book. That keeps the edit and compile loop fast while you are
working inside one chapter.

`:VimtexToggleMain` switches the buffer to the full project and back, for
when you want the real thing with all its cross-references resolved.

Oil helps here too: `gt` opens a `.tex` file from the directory you are
browsing, and `gP` opens a `.pdf` from it in the system viewer.

Some of the bundle's snippets expect particular preamble declarations. What
they are, and a preamble that satisfies them, is written up at
https://nathanaelsrawley.com/noethervim/guides/latex-setup/

